\subsection*{Key species}

\emph{Brachyscome pinnata} appears to be constrained to the southern portion of the fan and appears to be fast disappearing(Figure \ref{fig:brapin}). Intensive modelling revealed that there was no support for subplot random effects and their inclusion was an impediment for robust modelling (causing numerous diagnostic issues).

Preliminary modelling (top-ranked model \emph{Year + scale(fence.dist) + (1 | Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.80), suggests the presence of \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} is declining \mytilde 12\% per year. 

There is no evidence that \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} is declining but rather it's presence maybe associated with a gradient extending from the fence (Figure \ref{fig:sonnov}; top-ranked model \emph{scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.40). However, the second ranked model was a null model thereby suggesting there is effectively no detectable pattern whatsoever (\emph{1 + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.33).


